Manual harnesses. None is a gate — nothing here runs in `pnpm test`,
because they drive the real compositor, the real notification daemon and
the real bluez on the machine you are sitting at.

They exist because these things cannot be checked any other way, and
each was shipped unverified once already for want of them.

    soak.sh [seconds]        default 1800

Hammers the notification paths that surround the one crash we have never
reproduced: bursts from one app (grouping, the drawer), singles from
several (the cap), a critical (never expires), an RTL card, hovering the
stack open and closed, panel toggling, and natural expiry — which is
where the revealer collapse happens. Reports the round it died in, if it
does, so a crash is attributable rather than merely observed. Writes to
$SOAK_LOG (default /tmp/wam-shell-soak.txt).

210 rounds clean on 2026-08-07.

    pointer.py <dx> [steps] [hold_ms]

Synthesises a left-button drag through /dev/uinput, for the banner's
drag-to-dismiss. There is no pointer-injection tool installed —
ydotool and wlrctl are absent and wtype is keyboard-only — so this
creates a virtual relative pointer itself, the same way ydotool does,
and destroys it on the way out. Requires membership of the `input`
group; you have it.

Position the cursor first, since the device is relative-only:

    hyprctl dispatch "hl.dsp.cursor.move({x=5200,y=300})"
    python3 tests/manual/pointer.py 600 15 150

Two things that will waste your time otherwise:

- Hyprland 0.56 parses `hyprctl dispatch` arguments as LUA. The familiar
  `hyprctl dispatch movecursor X Y` is a syntax error that prints only to
  stderr and silently does nothing, which looks exactly like a pointer
  grab by a fullscreen app.
- libinput acceleration decelerates a slow synthetic drag hard. Drag fast
  and far when testing a distance threshold; a 240-unit drag over two
  seconds travels far less than 240px on screen.

To measure a widget without screenshotting it, compare layer geometry
before and after: `hyprctl layers -j`. The banner drawer opening on hover
is 85px -> 249px.

    btpower.ts               bundle it, then run it

The bluetooth power toggle's debounce and its failure path. Bundle with
`ags bundle --gtk 4 tests/manual/btpower.ts <out>` and run the result the
way tests/run.sh runs the suite (redirected XDG dirs — it imports
lib/bluetooth, which opens the SYSTEM bus at import).

The debounce cannot be seen from the UI, which is the whole reason this
exists: `togglePowered` reads bluez's own Powered, and during a
transition that has not moved yet, so a refused second click and an
honoured one ask for the SAME target and end in the same place. What
differs is one D-Bus Set instead of two — so the harness counts them. It
stubs `Gio.DBus.system.call` for the WRITE only, never answers it (bluez
is therefore mid-transition for as long as the harness cares to look),
forwards the read-only ObjectManager lookup to the real bus, and aborts
before touching anything if the stub did not take. It leaves the adapter
exactly as it found it.

Why the second Set is worth preventing: bluez answers a duplicate
`Powered` write during a transition with `org.bluez.Error.Busy`
(measured, 60ms in), which the pane would report as "Could not turn on"
over a toggle that in fact worked.

PASS on 2026-08-23.
